n and the point of distribution. Criminal law is the sharpest instrument a state has, and this is exactly the kind of harm that justifies it.\n\nAssign escalating liability to platforms and services that resist transparency — not to those that build capable models. The regulatory design pattern is proven: SEC disclosure rules penalize hiding information, not trading. Environmental law penalizes unreported emissions, not production. Pharmaceutical regulation mandates adverse event reporting, not drug capability caps. Apply the same logic here. Platforms that strip C2PA provenance metadata, refuse hash-matching cooperation, or delay takedowns beyond a statutory window should face escalating fines. Platforms that embed provenance, cooperate with detection infrastructure, and meet takedown timelines should receive safe harbor protection. C2PA already has Adobe, Microsoft, Intel, and the BBC on board. Google DeepMind's SynthID watermarking is in production. The infrastructure exists. What is missing is binding liability that makes opacity expensive and transparency rewarded.\n\nThe detection arms race cannot be won by concentrating capability inside the same platforms we are asking to self-police. StopNCII.org's hash-matching system works, but adoption is voluntary and controlled by a handful of companies. Groh et al. found in 2022 that human-AI collaborative detection hits only about 65% accuracy against state-of-the-art fakes. That number will not improve fast enough if detection research stays locked inside corporate labs. Congress should fund open detection research at the level it funds cybersecurity research through DARPA and NSF — and mandate that platforms provide API access to third-party detection researchers. Decentralize the enforcement infrastructure or accept that platform self-policing will perform exactly as well as it has for the last decade, which is to say: poorly. Two key assumptions underpin this framework. First, that provenance standards like C2PA 