ional framework that evaluated candidates before standardization, not after. That is the model: publicly funded evaluation infrastructure, adversarial red-teaming with published methodology, binding thresholds set by a government body. The Accelerationist cited the precedent that supports institutional pre-deployment evaluation — not the market-first approach they proposed.\n\nThe fact-check finding that architecture-level watermarks collapse under image-to-image regeneration attacks does not weaken the case for provenance mandates. It demolishes the case for provenance as the sole developer-side obligation. A defense layer known to be fragile on day one is precisely the scenario where layered accountability is non-negotiable. If the lock on the front door can be picked, you do not abandon locks — you also install an alarm and screen who gets the key. Pre-deployment capability evaluation is the screening layer. Mandated investment in watermark hardening is the alarm. Criminal liability for stripping provenance metadata is the penalty for picking the lock. Any framework that rests its entire enforcement weight on a single technical mechanism the research community has already broken is a framework designed to fail. The Accelerationist's bright line — provenance yes, evaluation no — concentrates all enforcement on the one layer we already know is fragile.\n\nFID below 10 on FFHQ at 256×256 resolution is a gameable single metric, and building a regulatory threshold on it invites exactly the evasion pattern we see whenever companies can redefine what their products are to escape oversight. A developer can train to score above threshold on the benchmark while producing photorealistic faces in deployment via higher resolutions, novel architectures like flow-matching or consistency models, or domain-specific fine-tuning on face datasets the benchmark does not capture. The \"international ai safety report 2026 1\" documents that fine-tuning techniques can remove safety prot