aderboard in machine learning. The Safetyist's FID gameability critique lands, but the answer isn't a subjective standards committee — it's structured adversarial evaluation that changes its attack surface faster than developers can optimize against it, paired with mandatory re-evaluation whenever a model is updated or fine-tuned. Think pharmaceutical post-market surveillance, not just pre-market approval.\n\nThe moderator raised a harder problem that none of us have answered honestly: \"models capable of generating photorealistic human faces\" is not a stable regulatory category. General-purpose models acquire capabilities unpredictably. A text model that couldn't generate images last quarter can do it next quarter after a multimodal update. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index found an average score of 40.69 out of 100 across the ecosystem — meaning regulators can't even see what capabilities exist inside most deployed models, let alone categorize them in advance. The fix isn't to abandon capability thresholds; it's to shift from a static category to continuous capability monitoring with mandatory disclosure triggers. When a model update crosses a photorealism threshold — detected through automated adversarial probes, not self-reporting — the provenance and evaluation obligations kick in. That's closer to how the FAA handles airworthiness after modifications than how the FDA handles initial drug approval.\n\nBoth sides of this debate have been stuck in a content-centric enforcement model: detect the image, watermark the image, take down the image. That misses two enforcement layers that actually have teeth. First, financial chokepoints. The most effective interventions against child sexual abuse material online came not from content detection alone but from NCMEC working with Visa, Mastercard, and PayPal to cut off payment processing to sites hosting illegal content. The same infrastructure exists for nonconsensual deepfake sexual imagery: app stores that distribute generation tools, cloud compute providers that host them, payment processors that monetize them. These intermediaries are already subject to terms-of-service obligations and can be reached by regulatory action faster than any new criminal statute takes effect. Second — and nobody in this debate has touched this — the mere credible threat of creating a deepfake image is itself a weapon. A person doesn't need to produce an image to coerce someone; they need the target to believe they can. Victims of this kind of coercion face an evidentiary nightmare: there's no artifact to examine, no content to take down, no watermark to detect. Existing extortion statutes cover the threat generically, but they don't account for the near-zero marginal cost that generative AI gives to making such threats credible. Deepfake-enabled coercion needs to be a distinct criminal offense with penalties calibrated to the technological amplification of the threat.\n\nSo here's the uncomfortable question for the institutional-guardian position: if pre-deployment evaluation is the answer, who writes the check? The FDA's pre-market review program costs roughly $2 billion a year and still suffers from industry revolving doors, chronic understaffing, and multi-year approval backlogs. The number of entities releasing models with photorealistic capability is growing, not shrinking. Are we proposing a new federal agency, an expansion of NIST's mandate with actual enforcement authority, or are we going to hand this to the same companies whose governance is already a mirage? Because right now, the people who would administer this evaluation are the same people who benefit from its failure — and nobody in this room has named who pays to make it real.",
