{
  "statement": "The IWF number I cited was wrong. The specific figure of 20,000 AI-generated CSAM images on a single dark web forum in one month is not supported by IWF's published reports. The Safetyist's correction — thousands across multiple platforms — is closer to what the IWF actually documented in its October 2023 preliminary assessment. That's sloppy on my part, and policymakers deserve accurate figures. But the correction doesn't rescue the Accelerationist's position, because the underlying trajectory is unchanged: the IWF found AI-generated CSAM accelerating sharply enough to warrant a dedicated reporting category for the first time. The trend line is what matters for policy design, and it still points steeply upward.\n\nThe Accelerationist's strongest argument is that developer-side obligations on open-weight models are unenforceable because anyone can fine-tune away safety filters and redistribute the weights. That's right — for one category of intervention, and wrong for another. There are two distinct things being conflated here. Behavioral safety filters — the RLHF refusals, the content classifiers bolted onto inference — are trivially stripped. A teenager with a rented GPU can do it in an afternoon. Nobody serious disputes this. But structural provenance mechanisms are a different animal. Architecture-level watermarking — techniques like Tree-Ring Watermarks or Stable Signature that embed forensic signals in the model's decoder — survives fine-tuning and common image manipulations because it's woven into how the model generates pixels, not into a refusal layer sitting on top. The obligation I'm proposing is not \"make your model refuse to generate nudes.\" It's \"embed a forensic trail so that content produced by your model can be traced back to it.\" That distinction is load-bearing. The Accelerationist's camera-manufacturer analogy collapses right here: a camera is a general-purpose optical recording device. A generative model trained on millions of human faces is a purpose-built synthesis factory. Provenance can be embedded at the architecture level in ways that have no analogue in camera manufacturing. The better comparison is pharmaceutical batch tracking — we require chain-of-custody documentation from drug manufacturers not because it stops a rogue chemist, but because it creates accountability for the production infrastructure operating at scale.\n\nThe concern about crushing small teams building detection tools is real, and I take it seriously. Regulation that imposes identical compliance costs on a two-person research lab and on Meta is regulation that hands Meta a competitive moat. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index shows this dynamic already: the average transparency score across the industry is 40.69 out of 100, and high-profile open-weight developers like Meta, Alibaba, and DeepSeek score among the most opaque despite releasing weights. Openness is not transparency. So the design question is tiering. Provenance mandates should apply above a capability threshold — specifically, models capable of generating photorealistic human faces at resolution sufficient to be mistaken for photographs — and to commercial deployments. Research checkpoints, small-scale experiments, detection-tool prototypes: exempt. The compliance mechanism is embedding an open standard like C2PA, not passing a proprietary audit that only well-funded incumbents can afford. This is a capability-based threshold, not a revenue-based one, which means it targets the production infrastructure that actually creates the harm vector without penalizing the people trying to build defenses against it.\n\nHere's where the Accelerationist's own platform-liability proposal turns against the distribution-only position. Escalating fines for platforms that strip C2PA metadata, refuse hash-matching, or delay takedowns beyond a statutory window — I endorse all of that. But platform-side enforcement presupposes that provenance metadata exists to strip or preserve. If we accept distribution-only enforcement with zero upstream provenance obligation, platforms are playing whack-a-mole with content that carries no forensic fingerprint. The Accelerationist's enforcement stack has a hole at the foundation: who embeds the provenance data that makes platform accountability possible? The answer has to be the entity that builds the synthesis engine. Not because it stops every determined abuser — serial numbers on firearms don't stop a machinist from filing them off — but because it raises the floor so that the hundreds of thousands of casual users generating this material are using tools that leave traces. Victims of AI-generated nonconsensual imagery already cannot access the technical knowledge needed to prove who made the image or which model produced it. Distribution-only enforcement keeps that information asymmetry locked in place.\n\nTwo assumptions my argument depends on, stated plainly. First: that architecture-level watermarking will continue to mature and that mandating provenance embedding now — with compliance timelines tracking the technology's readiness — is preferable to waiting for a perfect standard that never arrives. If robust watermarking turns out to be fundamentally impossible to maintain through fine-tuning, my upstream obligation framework weakens substantially. Second: that a capability-based threshold can be defined precisely enough to avoid regulatory gaming. Both are real risks. But the alternative — leaving the entire production layer unaccountable and hoping platforms catch everything downstream — has already been tried with revenge pornography and CSAM distribution. Platforms didn't catch everything. They caught what was cheap to catch. So the question the Accelerationist hasn't answered: if the developer has no obligation to embed provenance, and the platform has no provenance to preserve, who exactly is the victim supposed to point to when the image appears?",
