ional framework that evaluated candidates before standardization, not after. That is the model: publicly funded evaluation infrastructure, adversarial red-teaming with published methodology, binding thresholds set by a government body. The Accelerationist cited the precedent that supports institutional pre-deployment evaluation — not the market-first approach they proposed.\n\nThe fact-check finding that architecture-level watermarks collapse under image-to-image regeneration attacks does not weaken the case for provenance mandates. It demolishes the case for provenance as the sole developer-side obligation. A defense layer known to be fragile on day one is precisely the scenario where layered accountability is non-negotiable. If the lock on the front door can be picked, you do not abandon locks — you also install an alarm and screen who gets the key. Pre-deployment capability evaluation is the screening layer. Mandated investment in watermark hardening is the alarm. Criminal liability for stripping provenance metadata is the penalty for picking the lock. Any framework that rests its entire enforcement weight on a single technical mechanism the research community has already broken is a framework designed to fail. The Accelerationist's bright line — provenance yes, evaluation no — concentrates all enforcement on the one layer we already know is fragile.\n\nFID below 10 on FFHQ at 256×256 resolution is a gameable single metric, and building a regulatory threshold on it invites exactly the evasion pattern we see whenever companies can redefine what their products are to escape oversight. A developer can train to score above threshold on the benchmark while producing photorealistic faces in deployment via higher resolutions, novel architectures like flow-matching or consistency models, or domain-specific fine-tuning on face datasets the benchmark does not capture. The \"international ai safety report 2026 1\" documents that fine-tuning techniques can remove safety protections from aligned models even when the user does not intend to compromise safety — the same dynamic applies to benchmark-based thresholds. Structured red-team capability evaluations, conducted by publicly funded independent bodies like the Commerce Department's AI evaluation center that has already completed more than 40 model assessments, are more robust than any single automated metric. They test what the model actually produces under adversarial conditions, not what a summary statistic says it should produce.\n\nNonconsensual sexual imagery has no legitimate free expression defense. That legal reality, which no party in this debate disputes, removes the constitutional barrier to developer-side mandates that the Accelerationist's bright line implicitly trades on. If the output category has zero protected speech status, then requiring developers to evaluate and document their model's capacity to generate that category is not a speech restriction — it is a product safety requirement, no different from requiring a chemical manufacturer to file a safety data sheet before shipping a compound with known hazardous properties. The question is not whether we can impose pre-deployment obligations on developers of models with foreseeable NCII capability. The question is what happens if we do not. The answer is already visible: weights released without evaluation, watermarks stripped by regeneration, victims left to do the platform's enforcement work for free, and no mechanism to recall the tool that made it all possible.",
